#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f7af1dba-6300-56ab-93d2-0f3d1b893dad" :question/math-academy-id "e-21809" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x^2+10x$ for $x\\in[0,6),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-6,6).$ What is the value of $f(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068497/assets/3d7a4a25652434612765ebef26c7c0956342611968008bc4908a4865297e047d.png)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "1ff70a32-336a-5ff2-bfe8-0df06eec1a7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21810" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068497/assets/20ec903a94cb720f0d2c50b427409e36705af1f9f226b5c6fb11f93f92f0dd49.png)" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\nb_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4x$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{8}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi x \\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\nb_n = -\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n}=\\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n} \\cdot \\sin(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8590e119-0a06-5d5b-8b1e-5a6edacfc4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (πx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164888} {:answer/id #uuid "f98c1985-dd30-5c3b-a5c7-b30d18fb8035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164889} {:answer/id #uuid "5517755f-5376-584c-bfd5-e36f4d34876d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (πx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164890} {:answer/id #uuid "63f41640-412f-57df-8aaa-92588874897e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98c1985-dd30-5c3b-a5c7-b30d18fb8035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164889} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4265b0-d9b8-565c-a28d-2142be502e9c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4f00093-5bf5-53bf-b020-6c033978ec9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(1 − (− 1)^{n}))/(πn)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164893} {:answer/id #uuid "d511eb65-fcb2-5653-8aee-87de44c4ed02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10(− 1)^{n})/(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164894} {:answer/id #uuid "0744d591-06a8-5134-93c2-364d44611dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10(1 − (− 1)^{n}))/(πn)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164895} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7fd1e6-79f7-5313-a944-32349963d83b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(− 1)^{n})/(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d511eb65-fcb2-5653-8aee-87de44c4ed02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10(− 1)^{n})/(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164894} :answer-field/id #uuid "68012043-0dd6-5b01-bdec-4d15aac25762" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37a89f3d-e2cd-5d21-bb53-6d99da952356" :question/math-academy-id "q-340629" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7190e045-32f5-5fe2-997f-dbed81c063a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357454} {:answer/id #uuid "e4662f77-7696-5157-9055-476b2d5663d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357455} {:answer/id #uuid "d823b040-01d5-54da-80a7-3d3839523dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357456} {:answer/id #uuid "da46502f-4ac5-5007-87d6-3537f1adcb8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357457} {:answer/id #uuid "b16a5a44-102d-5e57-bf96-e713df35f973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-3{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7190e045-32f5-5fe2-997f-dbed81c063a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357454} :answer-field/id #uuid "0da5df9d-b9ce-518b-a167-fd1072434473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "619d01bf-a212-564a-aaf9-fa51027ac4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340866" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,5),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-5,5).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-5,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(3(-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a4e147-893d-5167-9b5c-87aba46a8685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357461} {:answer/id #uuid "962bad94-4780-5d40-8783-197c0c1cfbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{x}^{2}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357462} {:answer/id #uuid "2197853a-eb0f-55a7-af3a-66b0aab4ed0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4|x{|}^{2}+3|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357463} {:answer/id #uuid "ea206419-f6d7-5790-ac6f-cfe236fee02d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|4{x}^{2}+3x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357464} {:answer/id #uuid "69f25c18-feee-5104-8b61-70db324832ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f25c18-feee-5104-8b61-70db324832ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357465} :answer-field/id #uuid "69ed3827-87f1-5ce8-8e47-657da5530e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc335071-5d23-5d98-b858-91f9ea6ed62c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340868" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-4{x}^{2}-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-4(-x)^{2}-3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(-4{x}^{2}+3x) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af78195-7cb7-578a-a365-cd5829587aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357468} {:answer/id #uuid "4782782c-fe27-5891-8566-e02868c4f366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357469} {:answer/id #uuid "498ec876-9b1c-533e-9485-7fc44b1ef3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|x{|}^{3}-3|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357470} {:answer/id #uuid "7cec45e8-ee75-5648-b0f5-527249860ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357471} {:answer/id #uuid "a277f27a-334b-50fd-a67f-1242de902d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|2{x}^{3}-3x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af78195-7cb7-578a-a365-cd5829587aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357468} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6c0a61-fe1d-5737-bc46-1bfe1a8c9543" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c9986f6-455d-58dd-ab28-d5ee5bd89b4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340871" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-2{x}^{3}+3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2(-x)^{3}+3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}-3x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:k