ld/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11d339cd-fd6e-5b04-b877-5e266abbcd65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357480} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f12986-7678-53c9-af61-db9b8910865f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c34511e4-c391-5078-b8b3-a04fa93212e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-340922" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f507569-dcd3-5e05-b7db-7b669f249974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(1-(-1)^{n})}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357486} {:answer/id #uuid "86db0ec1-de4f-58da-a632-c3cd25b7b9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(1-(-1)^{n})}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357487} {:answer/id #uuid "07a684a3-b675-5a55-ad98-e8e65c20cdc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357488} {:answer/id #uuid "a933e85c-8b10-55af-9b3c-95cdd70e9db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(1+(-1)^{n})}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357489} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fccebf-a72b-5fba-9f32-a1e6461ac17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0fccebf-a72b-5fba-9f32-a1e6461ac17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357490} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ced504-7588-5c35-9864-a3d5341abf7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c587c539-44e0-5aa3-9938-b12fa29ef0b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-340926" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e858c2c-77a2-5510-8d00-d70684676589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e858c2c-77a2-5510-8d00-d70684676589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357493} :answer-field/id #uuid "1559f4a4-aafb-5525-b562-2afbf72d9e22" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dabab92-3d7b-5238-8b01-823555b737d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341210" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-{x}^{2}+5x$ for $x\\in [0,8),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-8,8).$ What is the value of $f(-3)?$\n\n$f(-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,8),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,8), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-8,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-8,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-(-x)^{2}+5(-x)) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}+5(-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb921b37-0af7-5e45-a3df-c7e7204be2c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357496} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd20428-c3b6-5340-b88c-fed465f2a5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357497} {:answer/id #uuid "fbca60e1-82d8-5f36-9fdb-a79e86858a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi +2)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357498} {:answer/id #uuid "10b36acd-bb3f-5765-a43d-00bec04ac1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb921b37-0af7-5e45-a3df-c7e7204be2c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357496} :answer-field/id #uuid "752bd285-eed0-5fdb-9626-ab2ae238e1de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357495} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20a4e58-f33e-5925-80da-eac282e3fb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357501} {:answer/id #uuid "5c02e1b9-8a1e-5780-98b9-5d9001e3fe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357502} {:answer/id #uuid "c7edcaa2-7e37-50c6-8744-a492daaeed57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357503} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8520f7-7e62-5614-9537-10496331115a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20a4e58-f33e-5925-80da-eac282e3fb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357501} :answer-field/id #uuid "c47802f0-aa1d-5211-a840-15de060de3a2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f67b8637-4bf3-58b7-8d0d-483388efc50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341336" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-3x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-3x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[(-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{3}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{3}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2+\\frac{3}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)(-1)^{n}-2)=\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2).\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c43f418-2c6e-5bc2-bf4a-e8aa070f1282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi +3)-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357507} {:answer/id #uuid "2e13b837-4381-5713-bc98-972f2fef9780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi -3)+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357508} {:answer/id #uuid "48c85509-281d-55f3-8b3d-65a7049dbbca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(-2\\pi -3)-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357509} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3ed4d9-7ce7-56b6-8e38-2955ce72a53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e13b837-4381-5713-bc98-972f2fef9780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi -3)+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357508} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bf59f8-6ff0-5ad2-8501-a2d1951e11b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b48fb2c2-63f4-51b9-922e-bb1335d6cc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-341381" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-2x+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-2x+3\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[(-2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3+\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)(-1)^{n}-3)=\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi -3)+3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb6411d5-48ed-5c97-bb32-2ae27b8e9b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357513} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7e6b2a-a5c8-5460-8f64-1a48e8e2b6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb6411d5-48ed-5c97-bb32-2ae27b8e9b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357513} :answer-field/id #uuid "d00205a4-0c85-5c1d-9631-4227fda4bec0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e2cfe5b-fb22-538b-9922-148d7cee940d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341406" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,10),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-10,10).$ What is the value of $f(-6)?$\n\n$f(-6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,10),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,10), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-10,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-10,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =-(-6)^{2}-2(-6)=-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
